## What's already true
- **PostHog is installed** (`src/analytics.ts`). It captures pageviews, and a pageview carries the full URL, so `?ref=acme` on a link already shows up as a property. `README.md` says GTM isn't installed.
- **Traffic is about 40 visits a week** (`README.md`).
- **The repo has no pages or layout files.** I can't see where `initAnalytics()` is called, so I haven't confirmed tracking fires. That's the first thing to verify.
- **No vault or earlier decision pages** exist, so this scope contradicts nothing.

## Capability by capability
| Ask | What it gives you beyond today | What breaks without it | Proposal |
|---|---|---|---|
| Per-application links (`/?ref=acme`) | The core goal. PostHog already records the URL, so this is mostly a naming convention plus a saved filter. | The goal itself | **In**, nearly free |
| Slack alert | Instant "someone opened the Acme link". This is the "know when" part of your goal. | You'd have to check PostHog manually | **One question below** |
| Google Tag Manager | Nothing. It duplicates PostHog and adds a second script and consent surface. | Nothing | **Cut** |
| Dashboard | PostHog has dashboards built in. A custom one would show 40 visits a week. | Nothing | **Cut**. Use a PostHog insight. |
| A/B test of the homepage | At 40 visits a week, a test would take years to reach significance. It would also muddy the recruiter signal. | Nothing | **Cut**. Revisit at roughly 1,000 visits a week. |
| CRM integration | A link open identifies an application, not a recruiter. You'd be syncing anonymous events into a CRM. A spreadsheet of applications does the same job at this scale. | Nothing | **Cut**. Revisit if you track more than about 50 live applications and can identify people, for example through a form. |

I don't think the cuts need a vote. If you disagree with one, say so. The strongest case against my position is that you're using this site as a project to learn the tools. If so, tell me, because that changes the answer.

