Redis would give you a cache shared across instances, one that survives restarts, and one that isn't capped by process memory. With one instance, you don't need any of those yet. Leaving Redis out breaks nothing today. Adding it brings a new service to run, network hops on every read, and invalidation and failure handling, all for a problem you don't have.

